# EDGE-AI inference-node package set — NVIDIA Jetson Orin (aarch64).
# One atom per line; lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Appended by build-jetson-stage4.sh for the edge_ai (headline) and full roles,
# ON TOP of packages.list + packages-k8s.list. The inference service itself
# (Ollama, OpenAI-compatible /v1) is NOT emerged — it deploys as the SAME
# arch-portable `edge-ai` cluster service the x86 RTX host runs (kustomize
# manifests in specs/cluster; Ollama publishes arm64 + Jetson CUDA images).
#
# ── CUDA-on-Tegra reality check (the kernel decision) ─────────────────────────
# On the DEFAULT mainline kernel flavor there is NO CUDA: the GA10B iGPU needs
# NVIDIA's nvgpu driver + the L4T userspace (nvidia-l4t-* / JetPack .debs),
# which are EULA-gated NVIDIA downloads — documented, not automated (see
# specs-jetson/kernel/README.md + installer/TEGRAFLASH.md). They are NOT
# portage atoms and deliberately do NOT appear here; x11-drivers/nvidia-drivers
# is the DESKTOP dGPU driver and is WRONG for Tegra — never add it.
# Until the L4T flavor is validated on hardware, the edge-ai service runs
# CPU-only on this node (functional, slow) — the builder warns about this.
#
# What we CAN bake portably today:

# curl: model pulls / OpenAI-API smoke checks from the node itself.
net-misc/curl
# htop: watching a 7B model peg six A78AE cores is diagnostics, honest.
sys-process/htop
# Python: the edge-ai-analysis sidecars + model-mgmt tooling expect a python3
# on the node (the k8s workloads bring their own runtimes; this is host tooling).
dev-lang/python
